WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.330 --> 00:00:03.630
- [Narrator] Turkey is
an economic turmoil.

00:00:03.630 --> 00:00:05.960
People are queuing for
bread, cutting back on meat,

00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:08.450
and they're even fleeing
to other countries.

00:00:08.450 --> 00:00:09.283
Why?

00:00:09.283 --> 00:00:12.390
Inflation, which currently
stands at more than 21%

00:00:12.390 --> 00:00:14.600
according to government statistics.

00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:16.520
The US inflation rate by comparison

00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:19.560
is that a 39 year high of 6.8%,

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:23.338
nearly twice as much as
a global average of 3.5%.

00:00:23.338 --> 00:00:26.921
(Kadriye speaking Turkish)

00:00:29.630 --> 00:00:32.770
Unlike the US, Turkey
is an emerging market.

00:00:32.770 --> 00:00:35.000
It seen two decades of
broad economic growth

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:37.410
and an expanding middle class, but now,

00:00:37.410 --> 00:00:39.830
the Lira is in free fall
as the country reckons

00:00:39.830 --> 00:00:41.820
with President Regep Tayyip Erdogan's

00:00:41.820 --> 00:00:43.680
unorthodox economic approach,

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:47.033
which is contrary to widely
accepted inflationary wisdom.

00:00:48.030 --> 00:00:49.910
As central banks around the world respond

00:00:49.910 --> 00:00:52.450
to a stronger than expected
pickup in inflation

00:00:52.450 --> 00:00:54.770
and mid economic recovery
from the pandemic,

00:00:54.770 --> 00:00:57.520
there are lessons to be
learned from Turkey's approach.

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:02.530
In a nutshell, to prevent
the economy from overheating,

00:01:02.530 --> 00:01:04.930
central banks can increase
the cost of borrowing

00:01:04.930 --> 00:01:06.480
by raising interest rates,

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:08.500
which curbs the rate of inflation.

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:10.610
And this is precisely what is happening

00:01:10.610 --> 00:01:13.030
in most other emerging
market's central banks

00:01:13.030 --> 00:01:14.290
to great effect.

00:01:14.290 --> 00:01:16.700
But Turkey's central bank
has done the opposite.

00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:18.420
Under pressure from President Erdogan,

00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:21.030
who has invoked a religious
prohibition on usury

00:01:21.030 --> 00:01:23.490
and his argument against
high interest rates,

00:01:23.490 --> 00:01:26.310
the rate has been cut three
times since mid-September,

00:01:26.310 --> 00:01:28.423
which has torpedoed the value of the Lira.

00:01:29.260 --> 00:01:31.620
A rapidly weaken currency
pushes up the cost

00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:33.910
of key imports like food and energy.

00:01:33.910 --> 00:01:37.542
This is already hurting the
Turkish people's bank accounts.

00:01:37.542 --> 00:01:40.959
(Hulya speaking Turkish)

00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:46.820
In an October survey,

00:01:46.820 --> 00:01:49.540
more than 80% of the Turkish
public said the government

00:01:49.540 --> 00:01:51.313
was handling the economy badly.

00:01:52.300 --> 00:01:55.360
The crisis has prompted protests,
mostly from union members,

00:01:55.360 --> 00:01:58.036
and calls for Erdogan's
government to resign.

00:01:58.036 --> 00:02:00.869
(people rallying)

00:02:03.070 --> 00:02:04.250
Turkish banks so far

00:02:04.250 --> 00:02:06.930
have been able to withstand
the currency crisis,

00:02:06.930 --> 00:02:08.470
but economists are concerned

00:02:08.470 --> 00:02:09.930
that a further collapse of the Lira

00:02:09.930 --> 00:02:11.370
could trigger a bank run,

00:02:11.370 --> 00:02:14.220
or that Turkey could default
on its foreign debts.

00:02:14.220 --> 00:02:16.920
Erdogan meanwhile has said
he welcomes the depreciation

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:19.870
of the Lira to encourage
an export based economy

00:02:19.870 --> 00:02:21.840
similar to Japan and Taiwan.

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:23.410
He and other officials have insisted

00:02:23.410 --> 00:02:25.850
that the currency
fluctuations are temporary.

00:02:25.850 --> 00:02:26.910
Some economists have said

00:02:26.910 --> 00:02:28.640
that the strategy is likely to fail,

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:31.300
because inflation has
put pressure on exporters

00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:34.290
who now have to pay more for
energy and raw materials.

00:02:34.290 --> 00:02:38.050
Others doubt that Turkey's
inflation is even at 21.3%.

00:02:38.050 --> 00:02:40.550
An independent research group
estimates that in reality,

00:02:40.550 --> 00:02:42.920
the figure is more than 58%.

00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:44.370
Investors and economists fear

00:02:44.370 --> 00:02:46.810
that Turkey's embattled
economy could get worse

00:02:46.810 --> 00:02:47.993
before it gets better.

